  "title": "Khorsabad Gate 3",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/city-gate"
  ],
  "details": "<p>This gate has been tentatively identified as the <a href=\"../789265692\"><span>Adad Gate</span></a> (Beate Pongratz-Leisten), the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../21271258\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">B\u0113let-il\u012b Gate</a> (Andreas Fuchs), and the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../344172036\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">\u0160ama\u0161 Gate</a> (Laura Battini, Jamie Novotny, Alexander Sollee). Because there is no one-to-one correlation between Sargon\u2019s inscriptions and available archaeological evidence, the proposed identification of the ancient name of Khorsabad Gate 3 remains open to debate. Since one expects a grand, decorated entranceway to be associated with the god \u0160ama\u0161, Gate 3 might have been dedicated to \u0160ama\u0161, rather than to the god Adad or the goddess B\u0113let-il\u012b.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "This decorated gate is the northernmost city gate of the southeast wall of D\u016br-\u0160arruk\u012bn (\"Fort Sargon\"), the capital of the Assyrian Empire constructed by Sargon II (r. 721\u2013705 BC).",
